::STAT_U04_L02_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U04_L02_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.2
Question:
What does P(A | B) mean?
{
=A. Probability of A given B happened#The condition B is already known.
~B. Probability of A or B#That is union language, not conditional probability.
~C. Probability of A and B only#That is intersection language.
~D. Probability of not A#That is complement language.
}

::STAT_U04_L02_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U04_L02_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.3
Question:
If 12 of 30 students in Group B choose tutoring, what is P(tutoring | Group B)?
{
~A. 12 percent#This uses the count as a percent.
=B. 40 percent#12/30 \= 0.40 \= 40 percent.
~C. 30 percent#This uses the group size as the percent.
~D. 360 percent#This multiplies instead of dividing.
}

::STAT_U04_L02_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U04_L02_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.4
Question:
If P(A) \= 0.40 and P(A | B) \= 0.40, what is the best conclusion?
{
~A. B changes the probability of A#The probabilities are equal, so B did not change A.
~B. A is impossible#A has probability 0.40, not 0.
~C. A and B are complements#Equal probabilities do not make events complements.
=D. A and B may be independent#The condition did not change the probability.
}

::STAT_U04_L02_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U04_L02_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.2
Question:
If P(A) \= 0.40 and P(A | B) \= 0.70, what is the best conclusion?
{
~A. A and B are independent#The probability changed, so they are not independent.
=B. A and B are not independent#The condition changes the probability of A.
~C. A is impossible#The probabilities are not 0.
~D. B has no effect on A#The conditional probability is different.
}

::STAT_U04_L02_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U04_L02_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.3
Question:
For P(A | B), which denominator should be used?
{
=A. The total number of outcomes in B#The condition B becomes the total.
~B. Always the full sample space#That is not conditional probability.
~C. Only outcomes in A#The denominator is the condition, not the target event.
~D. No denominator#A probability is a part divided by a relevant total.
}

